<main id="main">
<section class="case-hero"><a class="back-link" href="/#work">← All work</a><p class="eyebrow">CASE 02 / CUSTOMER EXPERIENCE · SERVICE STRATEGY</p><h1>Making customer differences useful for service decisions.</h1><p class="case-intro">For Braskem’s Netherlands headquarters, a CX workstream explored how customer needs, purchasing behaviour and operational realities could guide a more differentiated service offer.</p><div aria-label="Case at a glance" class="quick-read"><div><span class="quick-label">Challenge</span><p>Make customer differences meaningful for service and account decisions.</p></div><div><span class="quick-label">Contribution</span><p>Connect interviews, customer journeys, blueprints and commercial data.</p></div><div><span class="quick-label">Delivered</span><p>Customer archetypes, operational blueprints and six evolving customer clusters.</p></div></div><div class="case-metadata"><div><span>CONTEXT</span><p>Braskem · Netherlands headquarters</p></div><div><span>MY FOCUS</span><p>CX strategy, research &amp; data analysis</p></div><div><span>SCOPE</span><p>B2B customer relationships</p></div><div><span>TIMEFRAME</span><p>12-month initiative</p></div></div></section>
<div class="case-cover source-cover"><button type="button" aria-label="Enlarge: Project phases and outputs" class="image-open" data-caption="Project phases and outputs"><img alt="Project phases and outputs" data-source-index="108" draggable="false" loading="eager" src="/assets/source-108.webp"/><span aria-hidden="true">Expand ↗</span></button></div>
<nav aria-label="Case sections" class="case-nav"><a href="#challenge">At a glance</a><a href="#decisions">Decisions &amp; project material</a><a href="#outcomes">Outputs &amp; outcomes</a></nav>
<div class="case-content">
<section class="challenge" id="challenge"><p class="eyebrow">THE BUSINESS QUESTION</p><h2>Which customer differences should influence the service we offer, and how should teams act on them?</h2><div class="contribution"><div><h3>My contribution</h3><p>My role connected customer experience, data analysis and strategy. The work combined customer and Account Manager interviews, journeys, blueprints and cross-functional discussions to support a classification that teams could use in their decisions.</p></div><div><h3>Collaboration</h3><p>Product Manager, CX Strategist, Market Analyst and Scrum Master, working with commercial and operational stakeholders.</p></div></div><div class="facts"><div><strong>12</strong><span>months of research and development</span></div><div><strong>6</strong><span>customer clusters in the model</span></div><div><strong>1,000+</strong><span>customers processed monthly</span></div></div></section>
<div id="decisions">
<section class="chapter" id="decision-1"><p class="chapter-index">01 / DECISION</p><div><h2>Start with the decisions the service must support</h2><p class="decision-summary">Define the service and commercial decisions the customer classification must support.</p><details class="decision-context"><summary>Read the decision context <span aria-hidden="true">＋</span></summary><p>The classification needed to inform everyday decisions across teams. The initiative considered customer relationships, service benefits and differentiators, commercial priority and raw-material allocation. Two workshops moved from a broad set of variables towards a more focused basis for customer grouping.</p></details><blockquote>Segmentation had to support decisions, beyond describing customers.</blockquote></div></section>
<figure class="story-image wide-image"><button type="button" aria-label="Enlarge: Customer classification workshop" class="image-open" data-caption="Customer classification workshop"><img alt="Customer classification workshop" data-source-index="109" draggable="false" loading="lazy" src="/assets/source-109.webp"/><span aria-hidden="true">Expand ↗</span></button><figcaption><span>ORIGINAL PROJECT MATERIAL</span>Customer classification workshop</figcaption></figure><figure class="story-image wide-image"><button type="button" aria-label="Enlarge: Service utilisation and adoption" class="image-open" data-caption="Service utilisation and adoption"><img alt="Service utilisation and adoption" data-source-index="110" draggable="false" loading="lazy" src="/assets/source-110.webp"/><span aria-hidden="true">Expand ↗</span></button><figcaption><span>ORIGINAL PROJECT MATERIAL</span>Service utilisation and adoption</figcaption></figure>
<section class="chapter" id="decision-2"><p class="chapter-index">02 / DECISION</p><div><h2>Connect the customer journey to internal delivery</h2><p class="decision-summary">Map the full customer journey alongside the internal work needed to deliver it.</p><details class="decision-context"><summary>Read the decision context <span aria-hidden="true">＋</span></summary><p>Customer journeys explored purchasing, contact channels, friction and post-purchase experiences. Blueprints added the work behind those interactions across Customer Service, Supply Chain, Logistics and Sales. Customer and Account Manager interviews informed behavioural archetypes.</p></details><blockquote>The customer experience depended on several areas working together.</blockquote></div></section>
<figure class="story-image wide-image"><button type="button" aria-label="Enlarge: Customer Journey" class="image-open" data-caption="Customer Journey"><img alt="Customer Journey" data-source-index="111" draggable="false" loading="lazy" src="/assets/source-111.webp"/><span aria-hidden="true">Expand ↗</span></button><figcaption><span>ORIGINAL PROJECT MATERIAL</span>Customer Journey</figcaption></figure><figure class="story-image wide-image"><button type="button" aria-label="Enlarge: Customer Service Blueprint" class="image-open" data-caption="Customer Service Blueprint"><img alt="Customer Service Blueprint" data-source-index="112" draggable="false" loading="lazy" src="/assets/source-112.webp"/><span aria-hidden="true">Expand ↗</span></button><figcaption><span>ORIGINAL PROJECT MATERIAL</span>Customer Service Blueprint</figcaption></figure><figure class="story-image wide-image"><button type="button" aria-label="Enlarge: Experience measurement" class="image-open" data-caption="Experience measurement"><img alt="Experience measurement" data-source-index="113" draggable="false" loading="lazy" src="/assets/source-113.webp"/><span aria-hidden="true">Expand ↗</span></button><figcaption><span>ORIGINAL PROJECT MATERIAL</span>Experience measurement</figcaption></figure>
<section class="chapter" id="decision-3"><p class="chapter-index">03 / DECISION</p><div><h2>Build a classification that could evolve</h2><p class="decision-summary">Make the segmentation updateable as commercial behaviour changes.</p><details class="decision-context"><summary>Read the decision context <span aria-hidden="true">＋</span></summary><p>The project’s K-Means model used commercial data to generate monthly segmentation updates. More than 25 variables were considered, with 10 prioritised. A simple upload flow used Excel exports from sources such as SAP. The model’s technical development was a team deliverable; my contribution centred on CX, research, analysis and strategy.</p></details><blockquote>A useful account strategy needs a way to respond to changing customer behaviour.</blockquote></div></section>
<figure class="story-image wide-image"><button type="button" aria-label="Enlarge: Customer archetypes" class="image-open" data-caption="Customer archetypes"><img alt="Customer archetypes" data-source-index="114" draggable="false" loading="lazy" src="/assets/source-114.webp"/><span aria-hidden="true">Expand ↗</span></button><figcaption><span>ORIGINAL PROJECT MATERIAL</span>Customer archetypes</figcaption></figure><figure class="story-image wide-image"><button type="button" aria-label="Enlarge: Customer groups" class="image-open" data-caption="Customer groups"><img alt="Customer groups" data-source-index="115" draggable="false" loading="lazy" src="/assets/source-115.webp"/><span aria-hidden="true">Expand ↗</span></button><figcaption><span>ORIGINAL PROJECT MATERIAL</span>Customer groups</figcaption></figure>
<section aria-label="Segmentation and service levels" class="slide-deck wide-image" data-deck=""><div class="deck-header"><div><p class="eyebrow">EXPLORE THE ORIGINAL MATERIAL</p><h2>Segmentation and service levels</h2><p>Presentation sequence preserved from the original case.</p></div><div class="deck-controls"><button type="button" aria-controls="source-deck-8" aria-label="Previous slide" data-deck-prev="">←</button><span aria-live="polite" data-deck-status="">1 / 2</span><button type="button" aria-controls="source-deck-8" aria-label="Next slide" data-deck-next="">→</button></div></div><div aria-label="Segmentation and service levels. Use arrow keys or drag with the mouse." class="deck-track" id="source-deck-8" role="region" tabindex="0"><figure aria-label="Slide 1 of 2" class="deck-slide" role="group"><div class="slide-label"><span>01 / 02</span><h3>Segmentation and service levels: 1</h3></div><button type="button" aria-label="Enlarge: Segmentation and service levels: 1" class="image-open" data-caption="Segmentation and service levels: 1"><img alt="Segmentation and service levels: 1" data-source-index="116" draggable="false" loading="lazy" src="/assets/source-116.webp"/><span aria-hidden="true">Expand ↗</span></button><figcaption>Original project artifact.</figcaption></figure><figure aria-label="Slide 2 of 2" class="deck-slide" role="group"><div class="slide-label"><span>02 / 02</span><h3>Segmentation and service levels: 2</h3></div><button type="button" aria-label="Enlarge: Segmentation and service levels: 2" class="image-open" data-caption="Segmentation and service levels: 2"><img alt="Segmentation and service levels: 2" data-source-index="117" draggable="false" loading="lazy" src="/assets/source-117.webp"/><span aria-hidden="true">Expand ↗</span></button><figcaption>Original project artifact.</figcaption></figure></div><p class="deck-hint">Drag with your mouse, swipe or use the arrows · Click an image to enlarge</p></section>
</div>
<section class="case-lenses"><p class="eyebrow">CONNECTING THE PERSPECTIVES</p><h2>Experience.<br/>Operations.<br/>Strategy.</h2><div><details class="lens-detail"><summary><span>01</span>Experience<b aria-hidden="true">＋</b></summary><p>Customer and Account Manager interviews explored the relationship, service expectations and buying experience.</p></details><details class="lens-detail"><summary><span>02</span>Operations<b aria-hidden="true">＋</b></summary><p>Blueprints connected customer-facing touchpoints to Sales, Customer Service, Supply Chain and Logistics.</p></details><details class="lens-detail"><summary><span>03</span>Strategy<b aria-hidden="true">＋</b></summary><p>Customer clusters supported differentiated service and account priorities, with monthly updates rather than a fixed annual picture.</p></details></div></section>

<section class="outcomes" id="outcomes"><p class="eyebrow">OUTPUTS &amp; OUTCOMES</p><h2>What the work made possible.</h2><p>The project delivered customer journeys, operational blueprints, archetypes and a segmentation model processing more than 1,000 customers monthly. These outputs supported account management and service prioritisation. Forecast improvements belonged to a proposed future phase and are not presented as achieved results.</p><div class="reflection"><h3>What I take forward</h3><p>Customer classification becomes a service design tool when it connects customer differences to the teams, processes and choices that shape the experience.</p></div></section>
